#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
HTTP gateway that routes incoming messages to a named Claude Code session.

POST /message
  Body (JSON): {"message": "...", "question": "...(optional display text)",
                "files": [{"filename", "content_type", "data"(base64)}, ...]
                (optional — e.g. an image a messenger gateway forwards; each
                file is materialized under MESSAGE_FILES_DIR and its on-disk
                path appended to the prompt so the session can read it)}
  Body (plain text): the message itself

GET /conversation
  Returns an HTML index listing all days with recorded conversations,
  each linking to /conversation/YYYY-MM-DD.

GET /conversation/YYYY-MM-DD
  Returns the conversation for that UTC day as a human-readable HTML page.
  Each entry has a stable anchor (e.g. #entry-1749567890123) so callers can
  link directly to a specific exchange.

Conversation tabs (dashboard chat threads, distinct from the per-day log):
  GET  /conversations                 -> {"conversations": [summary, ...]}
                                         Optional filters:
                                         ?kind=chat|edit|cowork|companion|all
                                         (default chat — edit-command, cowork
                                         and messenger-companion threads are
                                         hidden from normal lists) and
                                         ?project=<uri>.
  GET  /conversations/<id>            -> full thread {id,title,messages,...}
  POST /conversations                 -> open a new thread (body {message};
                                         optional kind: "chat"|"edit", project:
                                         <uri>, project_title). Ara answers
                                         asynchronously (poll the thread).

Projects (dashboard project pages):
  GET  /projects                      -> live card data (SPARQL over the life store)
  GET  /projects/item?id=<uri>        -> one project's raw Markdown + sha256
  POST /projects/item                 -> save an edited project file (body
                                         {id, content, base_sha}); 409 + current
                                         content on a concurrent change.
  POST /conversations/<id>/messages   -> user reply (body {message}); async reply.
  POST /conversations/<id>/read       -> clear the thread's unread flag.
  POST /conversations/<id>/archive    -> the thread bar's Archive (`muted`
                                         untouched: news brings it back).
  POST /conversations/<id>/unarchive  -> back in the list, `muted` cleared.
  POST /conversations/<id>/flags      -> the Threads page's swipe and tray: set
                                         {archived, muted, starred} (any of
                                         them, booleans). `starred` sorts the
                                         thread first in its list and nothing
                                         else. Archiving settles the attention
                                         item, unarchiving reopens it.
  POST /conversations/<id>/delete     -> erase the thread for good (no body);
                                         the user's own act, accepted only
                                         through the reverse proxy (403
                                         otherwise), never for a chat's
                                         companion (409). Leaves a tombstone:
                                         every later read, append, flag write
                                         or keyed open of it answers 410 Gone.
  POST /internal/conversations        -> a retinue agent opens a thread that needs
                                         the user's decision. Token-gated via
                                         CONVERSATION_BACKEND_TOKEN (header
                                         X-Conversation-Backend-Token). Optional
                                         {kind: "cowork"} for the MCP connector's
                                         audit trail, {quiet: true} to append
                                         without an unread badge or Web Push, and
                                         {context: "..."} — agent-only context
                                         stored on the message and replayed to
                                         every later Ara session in the thread,
                                         never rendered to the user (e.g. the
                                         exact reply command, with reply token,
                                         for a proposed messenger reply), and
                                         {key: "..."} — an idempotency key: a
                                         second create under a key already used
                                         returns that thread (200, with
                                         "deduplicated": true) instead of
                                         opening a duplicate.
  POST /internal/conversations/<id>/messages
                                      -> a retinue agent appends a message (with
                                         attachments) to an existing thread. Same
                                         token gate; same optional {quiet: true}
                                         and {context: "..."}. A non-quiet append
                                         un-archives the thread unless it is
                                         muted.
  POST /conversations/<id>/shell-actions/<action id>/outcome
                                      -> the dashboard reports what became of
                                         a shell action (body {state, error?}),
                                         stored on it for Ara's later turns; a
                                         sensitive text reported typed is
                                         deleted from the stored reply at once.
  POST /internal/conversations/<id>/redact
                                      -> a retinue agent redacts one of the
                                         user's messages (body {message_id}),
                                         on the user's request. Same token gate.
  POST /internal/conversations/<id>/flags
                                      -> a retinue agent sets {archived, muted,
                                         starred} (any of them). Same token
                                         gate. `muted` is what keeps a thread
                                         archived when new messages are filed
                                         into it; `starred` is the user's mark,
                                         set only on their word. Also
                                         {continuous_off: true}: the user asked
                                         Ara (by voice) to end continuous
                                         conversation; the thread is stamped
                                         `continuous_off` (now, ISO) and every
                                         device showing it in the mode leaves
                                         it on its next read.

The life store (behind the dashboard's sign-in; read-only):
  GET|POST /sparql                    -> SPARQL 1.1 Protocol: query=<SPARQL>
                                         (URL or form) or an application/
                                         sparql-query body, with default-/
                                         named-graph-uri, answered by the
                                         life store as it answers; an update,
                                         a SERVICE clause or a cross-site
                                         browser request is refused (403).
                                         Without a query a
                                         browser gets webapp/sparql.html, any
                                         other client a Service Description.
  GET  /docs/<name>.md                -> a framework doc, as Markdown — what the
                                         SPARQL page renders and runs.

News feed (dashboard news page; see scripts/news_store.py):
  GET  /news                          -> {"generated", "items": [...]} ranked at
                                         read time. ?scope=feed|read|hidden|all
                                         (default feed), ?limit=<n>. An item's
                                         "url" is an absolute http(s) link, or
                                         empty.
  GET  /news/preferences              -> {"markdown", "updated"} — the Herald's
                                         memory of what the user cares about.
  POST /news/preferences              -> replace it (body {markdown}); the user
                                         may edit their own profile by hand.
  POST /news/feedback                 -> one user signal (body {id?, signal, note?};
                                         signal: up|down|read|hide|note). Nudges
                                         that item now and logs it for the Herald.

Agent models (Settings › Agent models; see scripts/agent_models.py):
  GET  /agent-models                  -> {"agents": [...], "models": [...]} — every
                                         dispatchable subagent with its built-in
                                         model and the effective override, plus
                                         the offered model list (the picker's).
  POST /agent-models                  -> {agent, model} pins one subagent to a
                                         model ("" = built-in); {agent, reset:
                                         true} returns it to the deployment
                                         default. Reaches the next spawned
                                         claude -p session, no restart.

Push notifications (dashboard PWA; see scripts/push_notify.py):
  GET  /push/config                   -> {"enabled": bool, "publicKey": <VAPID>}
  POST /push/subscribe                -> register a PushSubscription (body
                                         {subscription, notify_archived}, or the
                                         browser's subscription JSON verbatim).
  POST /push/unsubscribe              -> drop one (body {endpoint}); idempotent.

Messenger chats (the deterministic chat mirror; see scripts/chat_state.py):
  GET  /chats                         -> {"generated", "chats": [ChatSummary],
                                         "counts": {unread, read, archived}}
                                         — every chat in the message ledgers,
                                         SPARQL over the life store merged with
                                         the live overlay and the chat state,
                                         ordered by last activity. Each chat
                                         states its `list_group` ("unread" |
                                         "read" | "archived"; `group` is the
                                         boolean "a group chat"), `unread` —
                                         the inbound messages the user has
                                         neither answered nor marked read —
                                         and `inbound_seq`, the count of
                                         arrivals this view of the chat
                                         includes (what Mark as read hands
                                         back as `seen`); `counts` are chats
                                         per group.
  GET  /chats/<id>/messages           -> {"generated", "chat": ChatSummary,
                                         "messages": [Message]} ascending, the
                                         last CHAT_PAGE_MESSAGES by default.
                                         ?before=<ts> pages older history (a
                                         contract addition over the fixture:
                                         the fixture documents were unpaged).
                                         <id> is <channel>:<chat-key>,
                                         percent-encoded; the key is split off
                                         at the FIRST colon. A mail thread
                                         (channel `email`, docs/mail-chats.md)
                                         is a chat here too: named by its
                                         subject, with `participants` and
                                         `peer`; its messages carry `subject`,
                                         `recipients`, `cc`, `reply_to`,
                                         `trim`, `removed` and `original`
                                         — and `refused`, on a mail of the
                                         user's that the mail server took
                                         for only some recipients: those it
                                         did not reach. Its summary states
                                         `can_send` (false, with
                                         `cannot_send_reason`, where its
                                         gateway is unknown, not connected,
                                         or reports that it cannot send).
                                         Top-level `pending`: the sends of
                                         this chat that are not over
                                         (Pending: request_id, approval_url
                                         — a path of this origin — text, ts,
                                         author, to, cc, subject; `status`
                                         "pending" | "sending" | "failed",
                                         with `error` and `outcome_unknown`
                                         for a failed one, and `refused`),
                                         oldest first; [] on every other
                                         channel. A ChatSummary carries
                                         their count as `pending`, and a
                                         new mail that is only queued is
                                         listed by it (`last.pending`, with
                                         that send's `last.status`; where it
                                         failed, `last.failed` and
                                         `last.outcome_unknown`).
  GET  /chats/<id>/reply-preview      -> to whom a reply in a mail thread
                                         goes right now, as its gateway
                                         addresses it: {all, sender: {to,
                                         cc: [{address, name, contact,
                                         contact_id}]}, choice, subject}.
                                         409 {error} where no reply is
                                         possible.
  POST /chats/<id>/pending/<rid>/dismiss
                                      -> take a failed send out of the
                                         chat: 200 {id, dismissed, pending}.
                                         Same-origin only.
  GET  /chats/compose                 -> what a new conversation can be
                                         started on: {"channels": [{channel,
                                         label, accounts}]} — today e-mail.
  POST /chats/compose                 -> a new mail: {channel: "email",
                                         account?, to: [..], cc?, subject,
                                         text, request_key?}. 200 {id,
                                         message} when sent, 202 {id,
                                         pending} when the send policy
                                         queued it, 202 {unconfirmed: true,
                                         request_key, id?, message_id?}
                                         when the outcome is not known —
                                         never an error for a mail that may
                                         have gone out — and {error, sent:
                                         false, by, request_key} (424, 502,
                                         503 or the gateway's 4xx) for one
                                         that certainly did not; every
                                         refusal carries `by`, as a reply's.
                                         Gated like a send (same-origin,
                                         through the reverse proxy).
  GET  /chats/<id>/messages/<mid>/original
                                      -> a mail's original: {id, headers,
                                         header_block, text, html_url,
                                         attachments}. `<mid>` is a token from
                                         the message's `original`. 404 for a
                                         mail that is not that chat's.
  GET  /chats/<id>/messages/<mid>/original.html
                                      -> its HTML part, sanitised, as a
                                         sandboxed document under a policy of
                                         its own (never the dashboard's).
  POST /chats/<id>/messages/<mid>/trim
                                      -> the model trim of a mail the
                                         deterministic trim left `wanting`,
                                         asked for by the page once the
                                         bubble has been in view: 200 {state:
                                         "model"|"deterministic", text} when
                                         settled, 202 {state: "queued"|
                                         "running"} while a turn is due
                                         (asking again never queues twice),
                                         200 {state: "wanting", retry_after}
                                         when nothing was started (off, the
                                         day's budget spent, the queue full,
                                         backing off). Mail chats only (404
                                         otherwise); same-origin.
  POST /chats/<id>/read             -> Mark as read. Body {seen}: the
                                         `inbound_seq` of the summary the
                                         client showed; a message that landed
                                         since refuses the mark — 409 with
                                         {id, last_read, unread, list_group},
                                         nothing written. Body {} reads
                                         whatever has landed, {ts} only up to
                                         that instant. The mark moves forward,
                                         never past this clock (a message
                                         stamped up to two minutes ahead of
                                         it is covered beside the mark), and
                                         settles the chat's attention item;
                                         answers {id, last_read, previous,
                                         unread, list_group}. Body {restore,
                                         marked} undoes it — the mark goes
                                         back to `restore` only while it is
                                         still `marked` (409 otherwise).
                                         Opening a chat reads nothing. A mark
                                         that leaves nothing unread is passed
                                         on to the chat's gateway (POST
                                         /chats/read: a mailbox sets its mails
                                         seen), best-effort — owed and
                                         retried where it did not go through;
                                         the undo calls nothing. Same-origin
                                         only (403 cross-site), like a send:
                                         it reaches the real mailbox.
  POST /chats/<id>/flags              -> set {archived, muted, starred} (any of
                                         them, booleans). `starred` sorts the
                                         chat first in its list, nothing else.
                                         Archived leaves the active
                                         list; a new message brings it back
                                         unless it is muted, so hiding a chat
                                         for good is both together. Archiving
                                         settles the chat's attention item,
                                         unarchiving reopens it; archiving is
                                         passed on to the chat's gateway (POST
                                         /chats/archive: a mailbox moves the
                                         thread out of its inbox),
                                         unarchiving is not. Same-origin
                                         only (403 cross-site). Independent
                                         of the triage delivery gate: whether a
                                         group is a news source, or costs a
                                         model turn, is that policy's business.
  POST /chats/<id>/spam               -> mark a mail thread as spam (no body):
                                         its gateway moves the thread's
                                         inbound mails in INBOX and the
                                         archive to the mailbox's Junk folder
                                         (POST /chats/spam) and only once it
                                         has, the chat is muted and archived
                                         (its attention item settled). On a
                                         failure nothing changes here and
                                         `error` says why in one sentence.
                                         Mail chats only (400 for a messenger
                                         chat). Same-origin and through the
                                         reverse proxy only, like delete.
  POST /chats/<id>/contact            -> the contact card: {name, sphere?,
                                         tags?, permit?, chamber?, person?}.
                                         Files the chat's peer as a person in
                                         a chamber (a new one needs `chamber`;
                                         `person` links an existing contact),
                                         teaches the attention profile where
                                         they belong and re-judges the open
                                         item. An empty name unlinks the
                                         handle and puts them back into
                                         screening.
  GET  /contacts                      -> the address book (scripts/contacts.py):
                                         {locations, default, contacts}.
                                         ?q=<text> searches names and e-mail
                                         addresses; ?channel=&handle= looks
                                         a handle up (email, sms, signal, …).
  POST /contacts                      -> create a contact: {chamber, name,
                                         handles: [{channel, handle}],
                                         sphere?, tags?, importance?,
                                         permits?, vip?, instruction?}.
  POST /contacts/<id>                 -> change one: {name?, sphere?, tags?,
                                         importance?, permits?: [mode], vip?,
                                         instruction? ("" removes it),
                                         add?: [handle], remove?: [handle]}.
  POST /chats/<id>/delete             -> erase the chat for good: its ledger
                                         records and media (via every inbox
                                         gateway of the channel), the gateways'
                                         pending-send and recent-sender traces,
                                         the chat state and the companion
                                         thread. A later message from the peer
                                         starts a new chat. A mail thread's
                                         mails go to its mailbox's Trash
                                         first (nothing is expunged).
                                         Accepted only
                                         through the reverse proxy (403
                                         otherwise, like /send); 503 without
                                         CHAT_ERASE_TOKEN; 502 if the messages
                                         were not completely erased.
  POST /chats/<id>/draft              -> write the shared draft (body {text,
                                         version}); 409 + current state on a
                                         stale version; empty text clears it.
  POST /chats/<id>/send               -> send {text} through the chat's own
                                         gateway as the user (author "user" —
                                         direct under every policy category:
                                         the authenticated send press IS the
                                         approval `verify` exists for). Returns
                                         the sent Message. A mail chat takes
                                         `reply`: "all" | "sender", and is
                                         the exception: the press is passed
                                         on as `user_approved` and a mail
                                         the policy still queues stays
                                         queued — 202 {pending}. A mail
                                         whose outcome is not known (a slow
                                         or lost answer of its gateway, an
                                         SMTP answer nobody saw) is 202
                                         {unconfirmed: true, request_key,
                                         message_id?, since}, never a 4xx
                                         or 5xx. A mail that certainly did
                                         not go is {error, sent: false, by,
                                         request_key}: 424 where its
                                         gateway took the request and the
                                         mail server would not have the
                                         mail, 502 where the gateway was
                                         never reached, 503 where it cannot
                                         send. `by` says who said so —
                                         "gateway" (the mail gateway's own
                                         word, which ends what the key
                                         names) or "dashboard" (this side,
                                         which says nothing of the key).
                                         `request_key` (body) is the
                                         press's idempotency key: a repeat
                                         of it sends nothing twice, and is
                                         only reconciled (nothing read; the
                                         draft cleared only where it still
                                         holds that press's words).
  POST /chats/<id>/companion          -> {"id"} the chat's companion thread —
                                         the conversation where the user works
                                         out a reply with Ara. Creates it on
                                         the first call (201) and returns the
                                         same one afterwards (200); the id is
                                         also the ChatSummary's `companion`.
                                         An ordinary conversation of kind
                                         "companion", so the client drives it
                                         entirely through /conversations/<id>.
  GET  /chats/media/<slug>/<media-id> -> authenticated proxy for ledger media
                                         (the gateways' token-gated /media/<id>).
                                         Served in place only as an inline-safe
                                         image, audio, video, PDF or plain
                                         text; anything else is a download.
  POST /internal/chats/inbound        -> the gateways' notify rail: one message
                                         event (arrival or own-device echo).
                                         Feeds the overlay, keeps chat state,
                                         Web-Pushes arrivals. A messenger event
                                         is open unless CHATS_INGEST_TOKEN is
                                         set (news-rail model); an e-mail event
                                         needs CHATS_INGEST_TOKEN, else
                                         MAIL_GATEWAY_TOKEN, and is refused with
                                         neither (_chats_ingest_authorized).
                                         Every /internal/chats/* and
                                         /internal/news call a browser marks
                                         cross-site is refused.
  POST /internal/chats/merge          -> a gateway's word that chats are one:
                                         {channel, account, from: [key], into}.
                                         State, overlay and companion threads
                                         follow; idempotent. Authorized like
                                         an e-mail event on the rail.
  POST /internal/chats/removed        -> a gateway's word that messages are
                                         gone from where they came from:
                                         {channel, account, chat, message_ids,
                                         live}. `live` 0 archives the chat
                                         (never erases, never mutes).
                                         Authorized like an e-mail event on
                                         the rail.
  POST /internal/chats/<id>/draft     -> an agent stages the draft (token-gated
                                         via CONVERSATION_BACKEND_TOKEN; see
                                         scripts/chat-draft.py).
  POST /internal/chats/<id>/verdict   -> the verdict of the turn a sender's
                                         standing instruction started: {importance
                                         0–5, due?, actions?: [str], flags?:
                                         [str]} (token-gated like /draft; see
                                         scripts/chat-verdict.py). Taken only
                                         while such a turn runs (409 otherwise);
                                         decides the held message's ring when
                                         the turn ends.
  POST /internal/chats/<id>/flags     -> an agent archives, mutes or stars a
                                         chat: {archived?, muted?, starred?}
                                         (token-gated like /draft; see
                                         scripts/chat-flags.py). The user's
                                         POST /chats/<id>/flags by another
                                         door: same semantics, same mailbox
                                         hook. No delete.

Session logic:
- Conversations are keyed by requester identity (the "on-behalf-of" field, e.g.
  the Signal sender). Each key gets its own Claude session, state entry and lock,
  so a conversation is serialized within a key while different keys run in
  parallel. Requests without an identity share the default "Web" key.
- For each key, if a session exists and was used less than its idle window ago,
  resume it with --resume <session_id>. Otherwise start a fresh session. The
  window is SESSION_MAX_IDLE_SECONDS (an hour) for messenger keys and
  CONV_SESSION_MAX_IDLE_SECONDS (a week) for a dashboard thread ("conv:<id>").
- A resume Claude refuses — the transcript is gone, which it is after roughly
  30 days — restarts as a fresh session instead of failing the turn.
- Total concurrency is bounded by a small worker pool (WEB_GATEWAY_MAX_CONCURRENCY)
  to keep CPU/memory and subprocess count sane on a personal box. The
  presentation lint has a *separate* bound (PRESENTATION_LINT_CONCURRENCY): it
  runs inside a request whose caller is frequently a spawned session holding a
  worker slot, so sharing the pool would deadlock the caller against itself.
- Every spawn first refreshes an access token about to expire, under the
  cross-process lock all framework spawners share (scripts/claude_auth.py,
  docs/claude-auth.md), so a session never starts with a refresh that races
  the scheduler's jobs or the remote-control session for the token rotation.

State is persisted in STATE_FILE (a map of session-key -> {session_id,
last_activity}) so restarts survive as long as the sessions themselves are still
valid on the Claude Code side. The deployment points it at the persistent /root
volume (like CONVERSATIONS_DIR); the /tmp default would drop every session on
each container recreation, which is exactly what an update does.

Conversation log:
- Every exchange is appended to a per-day JSON file under CONVERSATION_DIR.
- Set CONVERSATION_BASE_URL to the public URL prefix (e.g. https://retinue.example.com)
  to also include an "entry_url" (format: /conversation/YYYY-MM-DD#entry-{ts}) in
  each POST /message response.
- Files are stored in CONVERSATION_DIR (default /tmp/web-conversations/), one file
  per UTC day named YYYY-MM-DD.json.
"""

